# Hardware

This folder documents the hardware components used in the Smart Sea Belt prototype.

## System Hardware

The prototype is divided into three main hardware units:

1. Smart Watch
2. Boat Hub
3. Base Unit

## 1. Smart Watch

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| ESP32-C3 | Main controller and BLE communication |
| MAX30102 | Heart-rate sensing |
| MPU6050 | Motion sensing |
| SOS Button | Manual emergency activation |
| Buzzer | Local emergency alert |
| Vibration/Indicator | Local user notification |
| Battery | Portable power supply |

### Smart Watch Flow

```text
MAX30102 ──┐
            │
MPU6050 ────┤
            ▼
        ESP32-C3
            │
       SOS Button
            │
            ▼
           BLE
            │
            ▼
        Boat Hub
```

## 2. Boat Hub

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| ESP32-S3 | Central controller |
| BLE | Receives Smart Watch data |
| GPS / NavIC | Location awareness |
| LoRa Module | Long-range communication |
| Buzzer | Emergency alert |
| Display | System status |
| Rudder Actuator Interface | Controls the diversion mechanism |

### Boat Hub Flow

```text
Smart Watch
     │
     │ BLE
     ▼
 ESP32-S3
     │
 ┌───┴─────────┐
 ▼             ▼
 GPS           LoRa
 │             │
 ▼             ▼
Location     Base Unit
```

## 3. Base Unit

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Controller | Data processing |
| LoRa Receiver | Receives Boat Hub messages |
| Display | Safety information |
| Buzzer | Emergency notification |
| Power Supply | System power |

### Base Unit Flow

```text
Boat Hub
   │
   │ LoRa
   ▼
LoRa Receiver
   │
   ▼
Controller
   │
 ┌─┴───────┐
 ▼         ▼
Display   Buzzer
```

## 4. Emergency Mechanisms

The prototype includes two physical safety mechanisms:

### Flotation Mechanism

The system includes a controlled flotation deployment concept intended to provide additional buoyancy during an emergency.

### Rudder Diversion

The rudder mechanism is designed to support controlled vessel direction change during an emergency.

The rudder mechanism requires vessel-specific engineering, control limits, manual override and safety validation before real-world deployment.

